      <h2 class="section__title">Sustainability Certifications | What Each One Actually Means</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Certifications are the backbone of verified sustainability in fashion manufacturing. Without third-party verification, sustainability claims are just marketing statements. Genuine certifications require annual audits, documentation, and compliance with specific standards. This section explains what each major certification covers, who issues it, typical costs, and implementation timelines. Understanding these details helps brands select the right certifications for their market requirements and verify manufacturer claims.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        GOTS (Global Organic Textile Standard) is the gold standard for organic textiles, covering the entire supply chain from organic fiber harvesting to environmentally and socially responsible manufacturing. OEKO-TEX Standard 100 focuses on chemical safety, testing finished products for harmful substances. OEKO-TEX MADE IN GREEN goes further, verifying both product safety and responsible production conditions. BSCI (Business Social Compliance Initiative) assesses social compliance and labor standards. WRAP (Worldwide Responsible Accredited Production) certifies ethical manufacturing practices focusing on labor rights, workplace safety, and environmental management. GRS (Global Recycled Standard) verifies recycled content and chain of custody. Fair Trade certification ensures fair wages and trade practices for workers in developing countries.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Different markets prioritize different certifications. EU retailers increasingly demand GOTS for organic claims and OEKO-TEX for chemical safety compliance. UK buyers require WRAP certification for ethical sourcing, with GOTS becoming more common. US retailers are rapidly adopting OEKO-TEX as a baseline requirement, while Fair Trade certification appeals to premium positioning brands. Brands should verify which certifications their target retailers require and ensure manufacturers hold current, verifiable certifications. SDF Clothing holds multiple certifications across these standards, providing flexibility for brands targeting different markets.
      </p>

