  <!-- Section 4: EU Regulations -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">EU Sustainable Fashion Regulations | What Brands Must Know in 2026</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        The European Union is implementing the world's most comprehensive fashion sustainability regulations. Brands selling to EU markets must comply with new requirements affecting claims, carbon accounting, product transparency, and end-of-life responsibility. These regulations fundamentally change how fashion brands operate, with enforcement beginning in 2025-2027. Non-compliance risks market access denial, fines up to 4% of global turnover, and reputational damage. Our <a href="/custom-clothing-manufacturer">custom clothing manufacturer</a> services help brands prepare for EU compliance. This section explains the four critical regulations EU-selling brands must understand and prepare for.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        The EU Green Claims Directive, effective from 2026, requires all environmental claims to be verified by independent third parties. Vague terms like "eco-friendly," "green," or "sustainable" without specific evidence are now illegal in the EU market. Brands must provide scientific evidence, methodology, and data supporting every sustainability claim. The directive targets greenwashing by requiring substantiated, transparent communication. Claims must be clear, unambiguous, and based on recognized scientific methodology. Our <a href="/wholesale-clothing-supplier">wholesale clothing manufacturer</a> services include Green Claims Directive compliance support. This regulation forces brands to move from marketing fluff to verifiable sustainability metrics.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        The EU Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism (CBAM), effective January 2026, imposes carbon costs on energy-intensive imports including textiles. Manufacturing powered by coal-based electricity faces higher CBAM costs than gas or renewable-powered facilities. Bangladesh's coal dependency (approximately 60% of grid electricity) may increase costs for EU imports versus countries with cleaner energy sources like Vietnam (hydro/renewables) or Turkey (gas mix). Our <a href="/clothing-manufacturer-turkey">clothing manufacturer Turkey</a> operations offer gas-powered production with lower CBAM costs. CBAM encourages brands to source from low-carbon manufacturing and requires carbon footprint reporting. Fashion brands importing to the EU must calculate embedded carbon in their products and prepare for carbon pricing.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) for textiles requires brands to register and pay levies in each EU country where they sell products. Implementation varies by country from 2025-2027. EPR fees fund textile collection, sorting, and recycling infrastructure. Fee amounts depend on product category, material composition, and recyclability. Brands must track sales volume, material composition, and pay quarterly or annual fees. Our <a href="/clothing-manufacturer-vietnam">clothing manufacturer Vietnam</a> operations provide EPR registration support. This regulation makes brands financially responsible for their products' end-of-life treatment, incentivizing design for recyclability and use of recycled materials.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        The Digital Product Passport (DPP), required for textiles from 2027, mandates digital tracking of fiber origin, manufacturer, recycled content, chemical use, and care instructions. Brands must implement systems to capture and store supply chain data accessible via QR codes or similar technology. DPP enables transparency, supports circular economy goals, and helps consumers make informed choices. Implementation requires supply chain digitization, data standardization, and IT infrastructure investment. Fashion brands should begin DPP preparation now to avoid 2027 compliance gaps.
      </p>

