  <!-- Section 5: Greenwashing -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Greenwashing vs Genuine Sustainability | How to Tell the Difference</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Greenwashing has become rampant in fashion as brands rush to capitalize on consumer sustainability preferences. Vague claims, misleading imagery, and unsubstantiated marketing statements confuse buyers and undermine genuinely sustainable brands. The EU Green Claims Directive specifically targets this problem by requiring evidence for all environmental claims. Brands must learn to distinguish between authentic sustainability and marketing spin to avoid regulatory penalties and maintain consumer trust.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Common greenwashing tactics include using terms like "eco-friendly" or "natural" without specific definitions, highlighting one sustainable attribute while ignoring broader impacts, displaying nature imagery without substance, making vague percentage claims without context, and using green color schemes to imply sustainability. More sophisticated greenwashing involves selective disclosure, where brands report positive metrics while hiding negative impacts, or using self-created standards rather than recognized certifications. The most reliable indicator of genuine sustainability is third-party verification through established certification programs.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Genuine sustainability is characterized by transparency, specificity, and verifiability. Authentic brands disclose material composition with percentages, provide certification numbers for verification, explain their methodology for sustainability claims, acknowledge both achievements and challenges, and share audit reports or impact assessments. They invest in certifications like GOTS, OEKO-TEX, and BSCI that require annual third-party audits. When evaluating manufacturers and suppliers, brands should request certificates, verify them through official databases, and ask for documentation supporting sustainability claims.
      </p>

