  <!-- Section 9: FAQ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Sustainable Fashion Manufacturing FAQ</h2>
      
      <div class="faq">
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What certifications make a clothing manufacturer truly sustainable?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Key certifications include GOTS for organic fiber processing, OEKO-TEX Standard 100 for chemical safety, OEKO-TEX MADE IN GREEN for product and facility verification, BSCI for social compliance, WRAP for ethical manufacturing, GRS for recycled content, and Fair Trade for fair wages. Multiple certifications across environmental, social, and quality standards indicate genuine sustainability commitment. Single certifications are insufficient - comprehensive certification coverage demonstrates holistic sustainability approach.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Is GOTS or OEKO-TEX more important for sustainable fashion?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            They serve different purposes. GOTS covers organic fiber from farm to finished product, including environmental and social criteria throughout the supply chain. OEKO-TEX Standard 100 focuses on chemical safety in the final product. For organic cotton clothing, GOTS is essential. For chemical safety verification regardless of fiber type, OEKO-TEX is critical. Many sustainable brands pursue both certifications - GOTS for material integrity and OEKO-TEX for chemical safety verification. The choice depends on your material choices and market requirements.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How much more does sustainable clothing manufacturing cost?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Sustainable manufacturing typically costs 15-25% more overall. GOTS certified organic cotton adds 8-12% to production cost, OEKO-TEX processing adds 3-5%, BSCI/WRAP compliance adds 2-4%, and recycled polyester adds 10-20% to fabric cost. However, these costs enable 30-60% retail price premiums, making sustainability profitable for brands positioned in premium segments. The key is targeting the right customers and effectively communicating sustainability value to justify premium pricing.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What does the EU Green Claims Directive mean for fashion brands?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            The EU Green Claims Directive, effective from 2026, requires all environmental claims to be verified by independent third parties. Vague terms like "eco-friendly" without specific evidence are now illegal in the EU market. Brands must provide scientific evidence, methodology, and data for sustainability claims. This regulation targets greenwashing and requires substantiated, transparent communication. Brands selling to the EU must audit their marketing claims and ensure all sustainability statements are backed by verifiable documentation.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is the Digital Product Passport for textiles?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            The Digital Product Passport (DPP) is an EU regulation requiring from 2027 that textile products carry digital information about fiber origin, manufacturer, recycled content, chemical use, and care instructions. Brands must track and disclose supply chain data through QR codes or similar technology. This enables transparency, supports circular economy goals, and helps consumers make informed choices. Implementation requires supply chain digitization and data management systems - brands should begin preparation now.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How do I verify a manufacturer's sustainability claims?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Verify certifications through official databases: GOTS at gots.net/certify, OEKO-TEX at oeko-tex.com, WRAP at wrapcompliance.org. Request audit reports and check certificate validity dates. Look for specific claims backed by data rather than vague statements. Ask for transaction certificates for organic materials. Genuine manufacturers provide verification links and documentation readily. Be skeptical of manufacturers who refuse to share certificates or verification information - this is a major red flag.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What fabrics are most sustainable for clothing manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Top sustainable fabrics include organic cotton (GOTS certified) with low environmental impact, hemp with very low water needs, TENCEL/Lyocell with closed-loop production, recycled polyester (rPET) reducing plastic waste, and bamboo processed with OEKO-TEX certification. Conventional cotton and virgin polyester have higher environmental footprints. Fabric choice depends on product requirements and certification needs. The most sustainable option balances environmental impact, performance requirements, and certification availability for your market.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Can small brands afford sustainable manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, small brands can access sustainable manufacturing by choosing manufacturers with lower MOQs. SDF Clothing offers 300 piece minimums for sustainable production. While per-unit costs are higher, the retail price premium for sustainable products can offset this. Many sustainable brands start with capsule collections using certified materials and scale as volume increases. The key is proper positioning and marketing to the right audience willing to pay premium prices for verified sustainability.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is CBAM and how does it affect fashion brands importing to the EU?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            The EU Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism (CBAM), effective January 2026, imposes carbon costs on energy-intensive imports including textiles. Manufacturing powered by coal electricity (common in some regions) will face higher costs than gas or renewable-powered factories. Bangladesh's coal dependency may increase costs versus countries with cleaner energy. CBAM encourages low-carbon production and affects sourcing decisions. Brands importing to the EU should calculate embedded carbon and consider energy sources when selecting manufacturing partners.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Is Bangladesh sustainable for clothing manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Bangladesh has made significant sustainability progress since 2013, with many factories now GOTS, OEKO-TEX, BSCI, and WRAP certified. The country faces challenges with coal-based electricity affecting CBAM compliance, but certified factories implement water recycling, energy efficiency, and waste reduction. Bangladesh offers competitive sustainable manufacturing costs, though brands should verify individual factory certifications and environmental practices. SDF Clothing's Bangladesh operations hold comprehensive certifications and implement sustainable practices throughout production.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What Changed in 2026 -->
  <section id="what-changed-2026" class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">What Changed in Sustainable Manufacturing in 2026</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        The sustainable manufacturing landscape shifted significantly in 2025-2026. New regulations and market dynamics changed how brands must approach sustainability.
      </p>
      <div style="background: #0a0a0a; border: 1px solid #1a1a1a; padding: 2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <p style="font-weight: 500; color: #fff; margin-bottom: 1rem;">EU ESPR Digital Product Passport</p>
        <p class="section__text">The EU Ecodesign for Sustainable Products Regulation entered force July 2024. Textiles face Digital Product Passport requirements starting 2027. Manufacturers need traceability systems to generate these passports. GOTS 7.0 certified factories have the required infrastructure in place.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background: #0a0a0a; border: 1px solid #1a1a1a; padding: 2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <p style="font-weight: 500; color: #fff; margin-bottom: 1rem;">EU Green Claims Directive</p>
        <p class="section__text">Effective from 2026, this regulation requires all environmental claims to be verified by independent third parties. Vague terms like "eco-friendly" without specific evidence are now illegal in the EU market. Brands must provide scientific evidence, methodology, and data for sustainability claims.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background: #0a0a0a; border: 1px solid #1a1a1a; padding: 2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <p style="font-weight: 500; color: #fff; margin-bottom: 1rem;">UFLPA Compliance</p>
        <p class="section__text">The Uyghur Forced Labor Prevention Act establishes a rebuttable presumption that goods from Xinjiang, China are made with forced labor. Cotton sourcing requires specific documentation proving origin. Manufacturers must trace cotton origin to avoid UFLPA enforcement.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background: #0a0a0a; border: 1px solid #1a1a1a; padding: 2rem;">
        <p style="font-weight: 500; color: #fff; margin-bottom: 1rem;">CBAM Carbon Costs</p>
        <p class="section__text">The EU Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism imposes carbon costs on energy-intensive imports including textiles. Manufacturing powered by coal electricity faces higher costs than gas or renewable-powered factories. This affects Bangladesh's coal-dependent manufacturing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

